# Quarkus non-blocking: Mutiny event loop

Use **Mutiny** (`Uni` / `Uni.combine`) for non-blocking I/O. This **replaces coroutines**. Do **not** use coroutines for non-blocking work (`suspend`, `async`, `coroutineScope`, `runBlocking`, `Flow` as a substitute for `Uni` / `Multi`).

Quarkus runs a `Uni` pipeline on the **event loop**. Mutiny burns a node more slowly so one pod can keep more I/O in flight; downstream needs that same admitted-rate ceiling.

Compose independent I/O with `Uni.combine` so work starts together and `map` runs only after all succeed. Downstream calls must return `Uni`, not blocking types. `map` must be an in-memory transform only — it runs on the event loop; a slow `map` stalls that loop.

## Example: REST handler with parallel downstream calls

```kotlin
@GET
@Path("{id}")
fun getSummary(@PathParam("id") id: String): Uni<SummaryReport> =
    Uni.combine().all().unis(
        posClient.getPosDeviceTransaction(id),
        productClient.getProducts(id),
    ).asTuple()
        .map { tuple -> toSummary(tuple.item1, tuple.item2) }
```

```kotlin
@RegisterRestClient(configKey = "pos-client")
interface PosDeviceClient {
    @GET
    @Path("/pos-device-transactions/{id}")
    fun getPosDeviceTransaction(@PathParam("id") id: String): Uni<PosDeviceTransaction>
}
```

## Do not

- `@RunOnVirtualThread` on a method that returns `Uni` (or `CompletionStage` / `Multi`)
- Blocking APIs (plain DTO / `get()` / JDBC) where a `Uni` is required
- `.await()`, `.get()`, `Thread.sleep`, or other blocking on the event loop
- I/O, waits, or heavy CPU inside `.map`
